From: mh2lm clock work <clock@mh2lm>
Subject: [PATCH 0249] refactor(rpmh): move the ACTIVE executor mechanics into a module, keep the owner on the hardware

C3-03 asks for one thing: the ACTIVE path's register access, its existing IRQ
cooperation, and its trigger/complete/cleanup mechanics move into an internal
module, while the service that owns the block keeps the gate, the order, the
deadline, the Busy decision and the failure retention.  This patch does exactly
that and nothing else.  The set of words the driver writes, the order in which
it writes them and the words it reads back are unchanged - the point of the card
is that the mechanics can be addressed through a module without the bus noticing,
so anything the bus could notice is a defect.

The module.  SweetRPMhExecutor.h/.cpp is a plain C++ translation unit with four
static functions and no member state at all:

  - arm() reads the source status word once and says Ready when the selected
    completion bit is clear, Stale when it is already posted, Invalid when the
    word answers all ones.  The owner's stale-before-trigger diagnosis hangs off
    the Stale and Invalid answers, so a bit it cannot attribute still ends the
    transaction where it always did.
  - step() posts one queued word and reads it back, at the word's own offset, or
    at the source status word for a write-one-to-clear word - the same two cases
    the inline code had, in the same order, one write and one read per call.  It
    returns Advanced, Blocked or Expired, and it deliberately does not know what
    a deadline means: Expired is a report to the caller, not a decision.
  - verify() samples the whole slot before forming any verdict: the source
    status, the slot's Status word, then every command status at its stride.  The
    verdict is formed after the last load, so the shape of the sample never
    depends on the answer.  Invalid is decided last on purpose.
  - stageCleanup() stages the tail into the caller's array: engine-mode word,
    engine off, slot enable off, wait off, the acknowledge as a write-one-to-clear
    word, and finally the source mask with the selected bit restored to what the
    slot inherited.  It stages the five fixed words whole or not at all, and it
    appends the mask word only when the caller's bounded storage can hold it, so
    a caller never walks a half-written tail.

Ports, not providers.  The module talks to the block through a two-thunk Ports
struct and a SweetRPMhLayout of offsets.  It receives no provider, no memory map,
no timer, no lock, no log: the storage behind the thunks belongs to the owner
(portRead/portWrite forward to the owner's existing read()/write()), and the
layout is declared once, next to the offsets it describes.  That is the property
the host test gates on - the module compiles against nothing but the system
headers, and its object file has four text symbols and no data symbols, because
a data symbol here would be a transaction the module remembers between calls.

Slot arithmetic is part of the contract.  The ports context is the TCS array
base, so the slot-relative offsets are added to the selected slot base by the
caller and the array-global words (source status, source enable, acknowledge) are
not.  This is stated at the layout, because getting it wrong is silent: addressing
a slot word from the array base makes the second TCS look occupied, which turns
the owner's own emptiness check into a Busy refusal.  That is how the fixture
caught the first version of this patch.

What stays in the owner, and why.  TCS selection, per-class admission, the panel
fence, the IRQEnable value composition, the GIC arming, the deadline, the Busy
decision and every log wording are untouched and still in SweetRPMh.cpp, along
with finish().  The write-dead handler still prints the SNAPSHOT row with the
word and the readback step() observed, then still calls diagnose("posted-write")
and finish(kIOReturnIOError) - the module reports, the owner retires.  Nothing
about ownership moved: the executor keeps no map, arms no interrupt, cancels no
timer and takes no lock, and the one new row says so once per boot:

  SWEET RPMH: executor=module tcs=%u commands=%u open=%u hardware-owner=SweetRPMh

The "open" count is the number of command statuses the sample found without the
finished bits or answering all ones, so a ring can tell a clean completion from
one that arrived with an unacknowledged command even before the following
fresh-completion row.

Parity, measured rather than argued.  Before the extraction the display fixture
was used to dump the ACTIVE trajectory of six situations - one rail vote through
a fresh completion, five ARC stages of one display vote, and one cleanup whose
completion never clears - and the result was 839 records over those six
scenarios, writes included in order.  Those records are embedded in
tools/tests/test-mh2lm-rpmh-executor.py as literals and compared record for
record, with a per-scenario digest.  The comparison is deliberately against
literals captured before the change and not against anything the module computes:
recomputing the expectation from the module's own constants would let a change in
a constant silently redefine the trace it is supposed to protect.  Two negative
controls were run against the shipped test: swapping the sample order inside
verify() fails the mechanics part, and swapping the owner's stage order for the
source mask and the acknowledge fails the trajectory part.

Host evidence.  tools/tests/test-mh2lm-rpmh-executor.py compiles the shipped
SweetRPMhExecutor.cpp alone against a stub IOTypes.h and drives it with a fake
register file - Ready/Stale/Invalid arming, a posted write that lands and one that
does not, a write-one-to-clear word observed at the source status rather than at
the acknowledge register, a word that expires with its write still outstanding,
the four-way verdict including the two invalid shapes, and the cleanup tail whole
or not at all - then replays the six scenarios through the shipped owner and
checks the six digests, the record and write counts, the single ownership row and
the fact that the owner still announces its own ACTIVE acknowledgements.  The
display fixture now stitches the executor as its own translation unit, declarations
before the owner and definitions after it, which is how the product builds it.
The whole rpmh/display fixture set runs 15 of 15.

Device check.  One ring with at least one natural ACTIVE completion and the
ownership row above; the auditor judges the C3-03 ring on the completion rows and
refuses to grade a ring that carries neither.

No clocks, no votes, no new writes, no change of order: C3-03 moves mechanics,
not policy.  The display owner remains the only voter and the only writer in the
stage, and submit() is still kIOReturnNotReady.

